## What the experiment says as it runs: its steps, board and answer. These texts come with the experiment when it opens; its card is in dyno-diy.ftl.

dyno-diy-command-apply = No such step in the experiment.
dyno-diy-marks-say = We make the dynamometer like this: a strip of paper glued on a board, a spring of the set hung from a nail at the top, a wire pointer and a paper-clip hook at its end. Where the pointer stands unloaded, we put 0. Now we hang 100 g weights — one, two, three — and each time make a pencil mark by the pointer.
dyno-diy-marks-button = ✏ Marks from the weights
dyno-diy-marks-plan = marks from one, two and three weights
dyno-diy-scale-say = A 100 g weight pulls down with 0.98 N. With a ruler we measure where the marks fell. If they are evenly spaced, the scale can be divided evenly: we find how many millimetres go to 1 N and put divisions every 0.1 N.
dyno-diy-scale-button = 📏 Divide the scale
dyno-diy-scale-plan = divide the scale into newtons
dyno-diy-apple-say = We hang a small apple — about a hundred grams. They say it was a falling apple that set Isaac Newton thinking about gravity. His friend William Stukeley wrote the story down from Newton’s own words and it came out in 1752: the apple fell as Newton sat in thought. Of an apple hitting his head there is not a word — that was drawn in later. What will our dynamometer show?
dyno-diy-apple-button = 🍎 The apple on the dynamometer
dyno-diy-apple-plan = the apple on the dynamometer
dyno-diy-egg-say = And now — in a little bag — the egg we weighed on our home-made scales. Will our two home-made instruments agree?
dyno-diy-egg-button = 🥚 The egg on the dynamometer
dyno-diy-egg-plan = the egg on the dynamometer
dyno-diy-trick = Many mix up grams and newtons: mass is not force.
dyno-diy-answer-why =
    A dynamometer is a spring with a scale. A spring stretches in proportion to the force (Hooke’s law), so equal forces give equal gaps between the marks — the scale needs only two or three known forces, the rest is divided evenly. Weights give known forces: the Earth pulls 100 g with 0.98 N.

    Mass and force are different things. The apple has a mass of 100 g and pulls down with about 1 N — the unit named after Newton. Were the apple on the Moon, its mass would stay 100 g, but the dynamometer would show much less: gravity is weaker there.
dyno-diy-answer-say = The apple — { $value }. A hundred grams is a mass, a newton a force.
dyno-diy-answer-text = The marks of one, two and three weights — { $value } from zero: evenly spaced.
dyno-diy-answer-text-2 = 1 N takes { $value }, a 0.1 N division { $value2 } mm.
dyno-diy-answer-text-3 = The 100 g apple — { $value } (F = m·g = { $exact } N).
dyno-diy-answer-text-4 = The egg — { $value }; on the home-made scales it weighed { $m } g, and { $m2 } kg · 9.8 N/kg ≈ { $F } N: the instruments agree.
